The '''Secret Trade''' (Zongese: 秘密貿易) was a system of slave trading and slave-based plantation agriculture that developed in the Zongese colony of [[Sinchew]] in [[Lurandia]] from the late 16th century through the mid-18th century. The trade involved the kidnapping of peoples from [[Dagit]] and the [[Balausia Peninsula]] by pirates and mercenaries, who sold them as chattel slaves to Zongese planters in the New World. The system was termed "secret" because it operated in apparent violation of the Zongese Edict of 1500, which banned slavery on the mainland, though the edict's applicability to overseas colonies remained legally ambiguous.

The Secret Trade was initiated by [[Ong Sam]], a Zongese merchant who established a plantation in Sinchew after visiting the Burawi colony of [[Lacashe]] in 1572. Inspired by the plantation system he observed there, Ong Sam arranged for the procurement of enslaved labour through piracy and kidnapping. The system expanded over the following decades, supplying the growing plantation economy of Sinchew with enslaved Dagit and Marosian peoples. Though Zong authorities formally recognised the trade's existence by 1650, effective action to suppress it was delayed until 1680, when diplomatic pressure from the [[Sultanate of Maros]] compelled the Zong court to deploy naval forces against the slave raiders. Smaller-scale raids continued in secret until the 1750s, by which time the enslaved population of Sinchew had become largely self-reproducing through natural increase.

Modern historiography debates the extent to which the Secret Trade was genuinely concealed from Zong authorities. Some scholars argue that the Zong court tacitly permitted the trade to support colonial economic development; others maintain that the colonies' distance from the mainland allowed the system to operate with limited metropolitan oversight.

== Background ==

=== Zongese Edict of 1500 ===

{{main|Zongese Edict of 1500}}

In 1500, the Kingdom of Zong promulgated an edict abolishing slavery within the mainland territories of the kingdom. The edict reflected both philosophical currents within Zongese intellectual circles and practical concerns about the social stability of a slave-owning society. The prohibition applied explicitly to the mainland; the status of overseas territories was not addressed, as Zongese colonial activity was then in its earliest stages.

The legal ambiguity would prove significant as Zongese settlement in the New World expanded. Colonists in [[Sinchew]] argued that the edict's protections did not extend to the colonies, a position that the Zong court neither affirmed nor denied for over a century.

=== Colonial expansion and the Great Disappointment ===

{{main|Great Disappointment|Colonial history of Zong}}

Zongese navigators established contact with the western coast of the New World in the late 14th century, following the conclusion of the [[Jade Sea War]]. Early settlements were small trading outposts, focused on the extraction of precious metals and the exchange of goods with indigenous peoples. These early colonies were not slave societies; labour was provided by settlers, indentured servants, and indigenous workers under varying arrangements.

The [[Great Disappointment]] of 1564 — the encounter between Volisanian explorers and established Fosian settlements on the western coast of the New World — transformed the colonial landscape. The revelation that the New World was already being colonised by Fosian powers prompted a rush to expand territorial claims and extract resources. The Zong court encouraged migration to the colonies, seeking to establish societies capable of extracting wealth and sending resources back to Zong.

=== Lacashe and the plantation model ===

{{main|Lacashe|New Burawa}}

The Burawi colony of [[Lacashe]], established in 1516 on the eastern coast of Abyala, developed a plantation economy based on the labour of enslaved Kaftian peoples. The colony's sugar, tobacco, and cotton plantations generated substantial wealth, and the system of chattel slavery that underpinned the economy became a model for other colonial powers.

It was in this context that [[Ong Sam]], a Zongese merchant, visited Lacashe in 1572. Accompanied by other Zongese entrepreneurs seeking fortunes in the New World, Ong Sam observed the plantation system and the wealth it generated. He concluded that similar operations could be established in Zongese Sinchew, provided that a reliable supply of labour could be secured.

== Ong Sam and the origins of the trade ==

{{main|Ong Sam}}

[[Ong Sam]] (王三; 1545–1620) was a Zongese merchant born in [[Tangsoa]], a port city on the eastern coast of the Kingdom of Zong. He belonged to a merchant family engaged in coastal trade and, in the 1560s, joined one of the early waves of migrants seeking fortune in the Zongese colonies of the New World. Settling in Sinchew, he established himself as a planter and trader.

In 1572, Ong Sam traveled to the Burawi colony of Lacashe, where he observed the plantation system and the use of enslaved labour. The visit proved transformative. Ong Sam recognised that the wealth of Lacashe derived from the combination of fertile land, plantation agriculture, and a reliable supply of enslaved workers. Upon returning to Sinchew, he determined to establish a similar operation.

The constraint was labour. Unlike Burawa, which had access to enslaved peoples through the Kaftian slave trade, Zong had no established source of enslaved labourers. The Zongese Edict of 1500 prohibited slavery on the mainland, and the Zongese court had not authorised the development of slave-trading networks. Ong Sam's solution was to procure labour through irregular means: piracy and kidnapping.

== Operation of the trade ==

=== Sources of enslaved peoples ===

The Secret Trade sourced its victims from two primary regions:

* [[Dagit]]: An archipelago in the western Shendan Ocean, north of the Sanese Archipelago. The Dagiti peoples were renowned maritime raiders and seafarers, and their island communities were targeted by slave raiders operating in the region.

* Balausia Peninsula periphery: The coastal regions near the Marosian frontier, inhabited by Marosian peoples. These areas were on the periphery of the [[Sultanate of Maros]], with limited state presence and endemic piracy.

The targets were typically small coastal settlements and fishing villages, where inhabitants could be seized with minimal resistance. Raiders operated at night or during the early morning, seizing men, women, and children before escaping to waiting ships.

=== Raiders and intermediaries ===

The actual kidnapping was conducted by pirates and mercenaries, rather than by Zongese colonists directly. The principal groups involved were:

* Pirates operating in the Kendriyan Ocean and adjacent waters: These independent operators found the slave trade more profitable than traditional piracy, as the sale of human captives to planters in Sinchew generated reliable income.

* Mercenaries from [[Yanbaru]], [[Okaiken|Mitoyagi]] and [[Nakamizu]]: The Sanese archipelago states had long traditions of maritime activity, and mercenaries from these regions were hired to conduct raids. Their involvement allowed the Zongese planters to maintain plausible deniability.

The raiders delivered their captives to intermediaries in Sinchew, who sold them to planters. Ong Sam and his associates established the networks that connected the raiders with the colonial market.

=== The Marosian periphery ===

The Secret Trade operated primarily in waters and coastlines that were peripheral to the [[Sultanate of Maros]]. The Marosian state maintained effective control over its core territories but exercised limited authority over distant coastal regions. The prevalence of piracy throughout Fosian waters meant that raids on coastal settlements were not unusual; the systematic targeting of specific populations for enslavement was not immediately apparent to Marosian authorities.

This ambiguity allowed the Secret Trade to operate for decades without triggering a diplomatic crisis. Marosian complaints about piracy were acknowledged by Zong, but the specific allegation that Zongese colonists were purchasing enslaved Marosians was not formally raised until the late 17th century.

=== Sale and distribution ===

Captives were transported to Sinchew and sold at informal markets. The trade was not conducted through public auction houses, as in some Illypnian colonies, but through private transactions between planters and intermediaries. Prices varied according to the age, health, and skills of the captives.

The enslaved population was put to work on plantations producing sugar, indigo, and other tropical crops. The plantation economy of Sinchew expanded significantly from the late 16th century, generating wealth that attracted additional migrants from Zong and reinforced the demand for enslaved labour.

== Legal status and the Zongese court ==

=== The Edict of 1500 and colonial exemption ===

The Zongese Edict of 1500 banned slavery on the mainland but did not explicitly address the status of overseas colonies. This ambiguity was exploited by colonists in Sinchew, who argued that the prohibition did not apply to them. The Zong court, for its part, did not move to clarify the legal situation for over a century.

The court's inaction reflected multiple factors. The colonies were distant, and communication was slow. The wealth generated by Sinchew's plantation economy was welcomed by the Zongese treasury. Confronting the legal status of slavery in the colonies would have required either enforcing the edict — which would have disrupted the colonial economy — or formally exempting the colonies, which would have contradicted the edict's philosophical premises.

=== Recognition and inaction (1650) ===

By 1650, the existence of the slave trade and slave-based plantation economy in Sinchew was an open secret within Zongese official circles. The Zong court formally recognised the trade's existence in internal deliberations, but no action was taken to suppress it. The colonies continued to send wealth to Zong, and the trade continued to supply labour to the plantations.

Historians debate whether this inaction reflected tacit approval or simple neglect. Some argue that the Zong court was complicit, choosing to ignore violations of the edict in exchange for colonial wealth. Others maintain that the distance and decentralised nature of colonial administration allowed the system to operate without explicit metropolitan sanction.

=== Marosian pressure and naval action (1680) ===

{{main|Maros-Zong relations}}

By the late 17th century, the Secret Trade had become impossible to ignore. The Sultanate of Maros, having accumulated evidence of systematic kidnapping from its peripheral territories, formally protested to the Zong court. The Marosian delegation presented documentation of raids, testimony from survivors, and evidence of enslaved Marosians in Sinchew.

The diplomatic pressure coincided with the broader context of the [[An-Ming Understanding]] and the need to maintain the alliance between Zong and Maros. The Zong court, recognising that the trade threatened the strategic partnership, finally authorised naval action. In 1680, the Zong navy was deployed to patrol the waters between the Balausian Peninsula and Sinchew, with explicit orders to interdict slave raiders.

The naval patrols disrupted the large-scale operations of the Secret Trade. Raiding continued on a smaller scale, but the system that had supplied the majority of enslaved labourers to Sinchew was effectively broken.

== Transition to self-reproducing population ==

By the late 17th century, the enslaved population of Sinchew had begun to reproduce naturally. Children born to enslaved women were automatically classified as enslaved, following the principle of partus sequitur ventrem (the status of the child follows that of the mother). This legal framework, adapted from the practices observed in Lacashe and other Illypnian colonies, ensured that the enslaved population grew through natural increase.

As the domestic-born enslaved population expanded, the demand for newly imported captives diminished. By the early 18th century, the plantation economy of Sinchew was sustained primarily by the labour of enslaved people born in the colony, rather than by new arrivals from the Balausian Peninsula.

This transition contributed to the decline of the Secret Trade. Even as the Zong navy suppressed the large-scale raiding operations, smaller-scale kidnapping continued. However, the economic incentive for such raids was reduced, as planters could now rely on the domestic-born enslaved population for labour.

== End of the trade ==

The Secret Trade declined gradually through the first half of the 18th century. The Zongese naval presence in the waters between the Balausian Peninsula and Sinchew made large-scale raiding impractical. The self-reproducing enslaved population reduced the demand for new captives. Marosian enforcement in its peripheral territories increased, making raids riskier.

By the 1750s, the systematic kidnapping of Dagit and Marosian peoples had effectively ceased. Isolated incidents of piracy and kidnapping continued, but the organised trade that had supplied Sinchew with enslaved labour for over a century was at an end.

== Historiographical debate ==

=== The "secret" designation ===

The term "Secret Trade" reflects the historical understanding that the system operated in violation of the Zongese Edict of 1500 and was concealed from Zongese authorities. However, modern historians have questioned this characterisation.

Critics of the "secret" designation argue that the scale of the trade — involving thousands of captives over more than a century — would have been impossible to conceal from Zongese officials. The wealth generated by Sinchew's plantation economy was known in Zong, and the labour source that generated that wealth must have been equally apparent. On this view, the "secret" label served as a convenient fiction that allowed the Zong court to maintain the edict's nominal prohibition while benefiting from colonial slavery.

Defenders of the traditional view maintain that the distance between Zong and Sinchew, the decentralised nature of colonial administration, and the involvement of non-Zongese intermediaries (pirates and mercenaries) allowed the trade to operate with limited metropolitan oversight. The delay in Zongese action — until 1680 — reflected genuine ignorance rather than tacit approval.

=== Revisionism and colonial complicity ===

Some historians have argued that the traditional narrative of the Secret Trade as a rogue operation, conducted by colonists and pirates without official sanction, serves to exonerate the Zongese state from responsibility for slavery. On this view, the Zong court's failure to enforce the Edict of 1500 in the colonies, and its recognition of the trade without action in 1650, constitute evidence of state complicity.

Others counter that the Edict of 1500 was explicitly limited to the mainland, and that the legal status of slavery in the colonies was genuinely ambiguous. The Zong court's eventual action in 1680, they argue, demonstrates that the state was not complicit but rather slower to act than modern sensibilities would prefer.

=== Comparison with Illypnian slavery ===

The Secret Trade has been compared to the slave systems of Illypnian colonies, particularly the Burawi system in Lacashe. Some scholars note that the Zongese system began later and ended earlier than the Illypnian systems, and that the Zongese Edict of 1500 reflected a philosophical opposition to slavery that predated similar movements in Illypnia. Others argue that the practical difference was limited: enslaved peoples in Sinchew experienced conditions similar to those in Lacashe, and the Zongese state's delay in suppressing the trade mitigates any moral distinction.

== Legacy ==

The Secret Trade left a lasting demographic and cultural legacy in Sinchew. The descendants of enslaved Dagit and Marosian peoples constituted a significant proportion of the colony's population. After the trade's end, these communities developed distinct cultural traditions, blending elements of Dagit, Marosian, and Zongese heritage.

The history of the Secret Trade remains a sensitive topic in relations between Zong, Maros, and the modern states of the Balausian Peninsula. Memorials to the victims of the trade have been established in Dagit and in coastal regions of the Balausian Peninsula. The historiographical debate over the Zongese state's complicity continues to influence contemporary discussions of colonialism and responsibility.

